text:
maria: Hey team — customer on enterprise Dedicated reporting truncated final chunk on streaming completions when using our TS browser SDK + AbortController + TransformStream in a Cloudflare Worker. They say final JSON object is missing ~20% of the time. Repro attached: https://github.com/redwood/repro/ts-abort-transform :eyes:

kevin: Thanks Maria. Any common factors? Specific browser, keepalive, or middleware?

maria: Using Fetch + `new AbortController()` pattern. Worker sets `fetch(url, { keepalive: true, signal })` and pipes incoming body via `response.body.pipeThrough(new TransformStream(...))` then reads chunks into the SDK iterator. Happens on Chrome + Firefox (workers), not a specific CDN region.

jun: Hm — sounds like an AbortController race where the transform's reader gets closed before final chunk flush. We've seen subtle ordering with `ReadableStream` lock/release. Do you have a minimal snippet?

maria: Yep, snippet from repro:

```ts
const ac = new AbortController();
const res = await fetch(apiUrl, { signal: ac.signal, keepalive: true });
const transformed = res.body!.pipeThrough(new TransformStream({
  transform(chunk, controller) {
    // pass through with small async op
    controller.enqueue(chunk);
  },
  flush(controller) {
    // flush final buffer
  }
}));
const reader = transformed.getReader();
for (;;) {
  const { value, done } = await reader.read();
  if (done) break;
  sdkIterator.push(value); // simplified
}
```

kevin: Are they calling `ac.abort()` on navigation or timeout? Could early abort be clobbering the final chunk?

maria: They abort on route change / SPA navigation. But repro reproduces even without explicit abort if connection hiccups occur. Customer says billing shows double partial tokens occasionally — they get charged for a partial token then again after reconnect.

olivia: That billing detail is important. If iterator yields duplicate partial frames on reconnect we might be counting tokens twice server-side. Need to check resume handshake and idempotency keys. I’ll open a billing trace for the customer id.

jun: I pulled the repro locally. Quick experiment: if I remove `keepalive: true` the failure rate drops but still happens occasionally. Suspect two issues: (1) TransformStream flush not awaited before `reader.read()` hits EOF, (2) our SDK reconnect path may re-deliver suffix frames when sequence sync fails.

infra-bot: [deploy-notice] canary runtime 2025-08-12T03:00Z scheduled to include stream-reader EOF fix.

kevin: Good catch. Jun, can you add an integration test that simulates an abrupt `controller.terminate()` and confirms SDK iterator ends with single final JSON? Also add a repro for duplicate token count on reconnect.

jun: On it. Proposed mitigation: SDK should buffer last partial frame and only emit completed JSON objects; server should return explicit final `eof_seq` in the stream metadata to make resume idempotent. I'll sketch a patch for TS SDK and add a Go/Python parity check.

maria: Customer escalated to Priority 2 because it's affecting billing and UX. Olivia, please share the billing trace when you have it and I'll coordinate a customer-facing workaround (suggest disabling keepalive and short debounce before abort).

olivia: Will do. Logged billing ticket #BILL-4821 with traces attached. Noting affected deploys: dedicated cluster eu-west-2 and eu-central-1 between 2025-08-10..2025-08-13.

jun: Created PR draft: `sdk-ts: buffer-final-chunk-and-handle-abort` with unit tests replicating TransformStream edgecases. Will also add e2e that pumps a partial JSON, simulates network reset, then reenacts reconnect handshake to ensure we don't double-count tokens.

kevin: Thanks. Maria please keep customer posted: short workaround (disable keepalive or add 200ms debounce before abort) until patch lands. I'll mark this for the next patch canary.

maria: Will do. Updating thread and pinging customer. :white_check_mark:
